    "result_text": "All 21 tests pass, including the new Cp12 (address-normalize) tests and the existing Cp10/Cp11 household tests. **BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (the sole `POST /api/owners` handler), applied whenever an owner is created.\n\n**Changes to `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n\n1. **`normalizeAddress` helper** \u2014 trims, collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands common abbreviations per whitespace-separated token via a small map (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). E.g. `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`.\n\n2. **Store & return the normalized address** \u2014 `addOwner` normalizes right after mapping and calls `owner.setAddress(normalized)`, so the persisted and returned `address` is the normalized form.\n\n3. **Required-field check on the normalized value** \u2014 if the address is blank after normalization, `addOwner` returns `400 BAD_REQUEST`.\n\n4. **Comparisons use the normalized form** \u2014 because the address is normalized *before* the existing household-duplicate detection and `householdId` derivation run (both via `householdKey(owner.getAddress())`), every address comparison now operates on the normalized value. This makes `\"12 Main St\"`, `\"12 main street\"`, and `\"12  MAIN  ST\"` compare equal for both duplicate detection (Cp10, \u2192 `409 CONFLICT`) and the shared `householdId` (Cp11, \u2192 identical ids).\n\nNo other call sites needed changes: creation flows exclusively through this handler. The whitespace-only reject case is caught both by the DTO bean-validation pattern and the new controller check, so it's robust either way.",
